        <div id="what-50-pint-means" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What 50 Pint Actually Means
          </h2>

          <p>
            The pint rating on a dehumidifier is how much water it removes in 24 hours under a standard test, and that test changed. Before 2019, dehumidifiers sold in the US were rated at 80 degrees Fahrenheit and 60 percent relative humidity. The Department of Energy's updated test procedure, which took effect for ratings in 2019, moved the test room to 65 degrees and 60 percent humidity, on the reasoning that most dehumidifiers run in basements, which are cool. Cooler air holds less water and gives the coils less to work with, so the same machine removes less in the new test and earns a smaller number.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical result is that the old size classes shifted down. What used to be sold as a 70-pint dehumidifier is roughly what is now sold as a 50-pint unit, the old 50 is roughly the new 35, and the old 30 is roughly the new 20, according to industry retailers and manufacturers who explained the change to buyers at the time. The hardware inside a 50-pint unit today is broadly the same class of machine that used to carry the 70-pint label. Nothing got weaker; the ruler changed.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Conversion</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">New 50 pint is roughly old 70 pint</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Ratings since 2019 are measured at 65 degrees and 60 percent humidity instead of 80 degrees. Same machine, smaller number. When you read an older review or forum post recommending a 70-pint unit for a basement, the modern equivalent is a 50-pint model.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            This matters for two reasons. First, older advice is still everywhere online, and someone told to buy a 70-pint unit may go looking for a size that barely exists any more and end up overspending on a commercial machine. Second, the newer test is more honest for basement use. A rating taken at 65 degrees is much closer to the conditions in a typical below-grade space than one taken at 80 degrees, so the number on the box is a better guide to what the unit will really do downstairs.
          </p>

          <p>
            One caution: the rating is still a lab number at a fixed temperature and humidity. In a muggy August basement at 72 degrees and 75 percent humidity, a 50-pint unit can pull more than 50 pints. In a cool spring basement at 58 degrees, it will pull less, and it will spend some of its time defrosting. Treat the rating as a way to compare machines with each other, not as a promise of daily output in your house.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
