        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best 50 pint dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most basements, the Frigidaire 50-pint dehumidifier with Wi-Fi is the best 50 pint dehumidifier, because the app alerts you when the bucket is full or humidity rises. If you have no floor drain, a pump model such as the Frigidaire FFAP5033W1 or FHDP5033Y1 is better, and the hOmeLabs is the best value.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How big of a space does a 50 pint dehumidifier cover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Makers often claim 1,500 to 4,500 square feet, and even single brands list both figures. Size by dampness rather than area. A 50-pint unit is the right default for most damp single-family basements; very large, partitioned or persistently wet spaces may need a ducted whole-basement unit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 50 pint dehumidifier the same as a 70 pint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Roughly, yes. The Department of Energy changed the test in 2019 from 80 degrees to 65 degrees and 60 percent humidity. Cooler test conditions produce lower numbers, so units that used to be rated at 70 pints are now rated at about 50. The hardware class is broadly the same.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a wifi dehumidifier worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a basement you do not visit daily, yes. A Wi-Fi dehumidifier shows room humidity on your phone and alerts you when the bucket is full, so a silent shutdown does not go unnoticed for weeks. If the unit drains continuously or sits in a room you use daily, the benefit is smaller.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a 50 pint dehumidifier with pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if there is no floor drain below where the unit will sit. A built-in pump pushes water up to a sink or out a window, so the unit runs without emptying. If you have a floor drain, a standard unit on a gravity hose is simpler, quieter and has one less part to fail.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What humidity should I set a smart dehumidifier to?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start at 50 percent. EPA guidance recommends keeping indoor humidity below 60 percent and ideally between 30 and 50 percent to discourage mold. Setting a smart dehumidifier much lower than 40 percent in a basement mostly increases run time and electricity use without much added benefit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a 50 pint dehumidifier work in a cold basement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It will run, but less effectively. Frigidaire lists its 50-pint units as operating down to about 41 degrees, but compressor units lose output and spend more time defrosting as the room cools below the mid-60s. For a basement that stays damp in the 40s or low 50s, a desiccant dehumidifier is the better tool.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

